    "body": "Fixes #7472\n\nIn the example from the issue, `(val > 0 and not a and not b) if switch_cond else True`, the parser nests `not a and not b` as its own `BoolBinopNode` with type `bint`, while the outer and/or becomes a Python object. Every operand writes into the outer result, but `BoolBinopNode._wrap_operand()` only wraps leaf operands, so the nested node keeps producing `bint` values. A later `coerce_to()` on the whole expression would repair that, but `CondExprNode` and comparisons skip it when the type already matches. The generated C then contains\n\n```c\n__pyx_t_2 = ((PyObject *)__pyx_t_4);\n```\n\nso a true value segfaults, and a false value becomes NULL, which crashes when used (as in the issue) or raises `SystemError` when returned. `(x > 0 and not a and not b) == y` crashes the same way, and so do nested C `int`, `char*`, enum and C++ `bool` operands.\n\nThe fix makes `_wrap_operand()` convert a nested and/or when one side is a C value and the other a Python object. Nested C values of another C type are already converted when stored, so they are left alone. Operands that cannot become Python objects, such as C pointers or `NULL`, are also left alone, because `if obj or (p1 or p2):` works today. Using such a pointer expression as a value still crashes, exactly as on master.\n\nTesting on macOS with Python 3.13:\n\n* New doctests in `tests/run/boolop_py.py` (conditional expression and comparison) and `tests/run/boolop.pyx` (typed C ints, and pointers, `NULL` and an enum nested in a `double` and/or, which compile on master). Without the fix both files segfault.\n* Related bool, and/or and conditional tests in C and C++ mode: 626 tests, all OK.\n* Generated C for 19 existing tests plus five of Cython's own modules is byte identical with and without the change.\n\nHappy to add a CHANGES.rst entry if you want one.\n",
